t(61, 2385, """In a comment written by a brother of ours here on the channel, the comment was about a noble brother, the owner of a channel, who told people that he is the one mentioned in the hadith of the Prophet ﷺ; most likely he means a particular personality, perhaps, since the commenting brother did not clarify.

O noble ones .. This is advice for the sake of Allah to every person, including the speaker; I do not exempt myself .. A person must know that the favor of Allah the Exalted is upon all the worlds .. upon every person and every Muslim man and woman .. and every believing man and woman .. and that""")

t(62, 60, """a person is between a favor and a trial, and the hair between them can barely be seen .. Favor from Allah the Exalted is established for all His servants, men and women, but trial also occurs and no one is exempt from it. So whoever receives signs of favor from Allah the Almighty our Lord, let him look at his own soul, what became of it through them, so as to distinguish between truth and falsehood in what came to him. And whoever receives signs and finds in himself pride, arrogance and haughtiness, let him know that they are not from Allah the Almighty our Lord, because favor is not given to an arrogant or a conceited person; what came was only a trial from Satan for a boastful arrogant one and was not from the favor of Allah the Almighty, so let him understand that. How many a tempted man walked in the street and was told words that tempted him. I saw that myself in a man long years ago; he was tempted in himself and no longer treated people except with superiority, arrogance and irritability. So was it a sign of favor or a sign of trial? Such too is the state of the owner of that channel, may Allah guide him, whom the brother mentioned in the comment; we do not know what was said to him as he walked in the street that he should be tempted like this and come out to people saying of himself openly that he is a specific personality. This comes from haste and impulsiveness, and from not assessing situations with wisdom. If phenomena are the cause of what happened to him, then I say on this matter a clear, plain word. Every Muslim man and every Muslim woman experiences states outside the door of the rational; they must place what happens to them in the pans of the scale of the heart, the scale of piety and the scale of justice in their lives, the scale of the love of Allah in their hearts and then the love of people, and the scale of their striving on earth for reform. If what happened to a person agrees with all of that, let him praise Allah; it is closer to glad tidings from Allah the Exalted than to trial. Because the one exposed to what he heard and saw was not tempted, and he is in his states a good man or a good woman, upon the face of truth, not illusion. But if he is tempted so that his states of worship change and he relapses, or he becomes arrogant toward people, then it is a trial without doubt. I too have states in this matter that do not go outside the circle of what befalls people of Allah's favor upon them so that their hearts may be tested. Do you think, my Muslim brother, that a single thing that happens to us in our lives is not a test? Then if you do, you have not understood the reality of the life of this world! I do not claim to be indifferent to seeing Allah's favor or not to rejoice in the favor of Allah the Exalted, far be it from Allah. But I place His favor, sanctified be His names, in strange phenomena under the heading of contemplation, reflection and self-examination, not under the heading of pride and self-regard. We seek refuge in Allah from Pharaoh, Haman and Qarun, and before them their first teacher Iblis and his successors on earth until the Day of Resurrection. When I spent months in Egypt, among the most beautiful of days, I witnessed two cases in its squares: the first was a man, not a majdhub, who passed by me at the Mosque of Sayyida Nafisa and said in a loud voice, “Peace be upon you, O sharif”; the second was a majdhub man from the people of Upper Egypt, may Allah protect its people, who passed by me and rushed toward me in a rush not logical by the reckonings of reason and shouted in a voice that all the passers-by heard, saying: “O light .. O possessor of knowledge.” In both cases I paused in reflection. Even so, it befits the Muslim to move on and complete his own way. He completes his way as it is. Humble as he always is; he does not feel in himself, Allah forbid, that he is something important and distinguished from people, he does not intend to place himself above anyone, and there is in his heart no desire at all to be above anyone. Otherwise it is trial and the ruin of religion, the ruin of the heart and the ruin of the heart's soundness, the loss of the soundness of the innate nature, and the reversal of conduct.

In the same context as this advice, we have a noble brother, a follower, who some time ago wrote a comment here and I answered him at the time, since he thought of me that I no longer reply to comments out of arrogance, Allah forbid. So I explained to him that the matter is as far from that as can be; rather, pains and aches have increased in a way the mind cannot imagine. By Allah, I do not like talking about this matter unless I am accused and so am forced to defend myself. As for what I suffer, I will not complain of it to people, but an accusation was made against me, so I had to remove the confusion about it. What holds me back is never arrogance, but pains, in the last 6 months in particular, in almost every""")

t(62, 1305, """place in the body, which go away only during sleep because the person is asleep. So even on WhatsApp, the brothers and sisters who contact me know that I do not reply quickly, and all of them, praise be to Allah, excuse it; the cause is beyond a person's will. The matter is not easy, neither at the level of the body nor at the level of the severe pressure on the mind, to the point that a person may feel that his mind is literally asking for death to be relieved of the strange pressure upon it. This is a clearing of my conscience before people; as for before Allah the Exalted our Lord, Allah is aware of everything.""")

t(63, 60, """I hope this scene never happens to the Arabs in Europe during the war .. For this, my advice is .. Pledge among yourselves from now to move out in groups .. Whoever has a car takes with him those who have no car.. And do not forget the absolute best food for ease of carrying and nutritional value .. dates .. the master of foods in time of wars .. And do not forget, each according to the nature of his geography, do not forget anything you believe will be useful to you .. Remember: the scene in this picture is very bad .. it is a scene of fleeing on foot inside forests! And its dangers are very high .. So work from now on putting your affairs in order.""")

